uctance in frog olfactory cilia</article-title><source>J. Neurosci.</source><volume>11</volume><year>1991</year><fpage>3624</fpage><lpage>3629</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1941099</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Kleeneetal1994"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kleene</surname><given-names>S.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gesteland</surname><given-names>R.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bryant</surname><given-names>S.H.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>An electrophysiological survey of frog olfactory cilia</article-title><source>J. Exp. Biol.</source><volume>195</volume><year>1994</year><fpage>307</fpage><lpage>328</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7964415</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="KleeneandPun1996"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kleene</surname><given-names>S.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Pun</surname><given-names>R.Y.K.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Persistence of the olfactory receptor current in a wide variety of extracellular environments</article-title><source>J. Neurophysiol.</source><volume>75</volume><year>1996</year><fpage>1386</fpage><lpage>1391</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8727385</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="KleymanandCragoe1988"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kleyman</surname><given-names>T.R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Cragoe</surname><given-names>E.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Amiloride and its analogs as tools in the study of ion transport</article-title><source>J. Membr. Biol.</source><volume>105</volume><year>1988</year><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>21</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2852254</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="KurahashiandMenini1997"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kurahashi</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Menini</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mechanism of odorant adaptation in the olfactory receptor cell</article-title><source>Nature.</source><volume>385</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>725</fpage><lpage>729</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9034189</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="KurahashiandShibuya1990"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kurahashi</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Shibuya</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ca<sup>2+</sup>-dependent adaptive properties in the solitary olfactory receptor cell of the newt</article-title><source>Brain Res.</source><volume>515</volume><year>1990</year><fpage>261</fpage><lpage>268</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2113412</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="KurahashiandYau1993"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kurahashi</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Yau</surname><given-names>K.W.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Co-existence of cationic and chloride components in odorant-induced current of vertebrate olfactory receptor cells</article-title><source>Nature.</source><volume>363</volume><year>1993</year><fpage>71</fpage><lpage>74</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7683113</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Leinders-Zufalletal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Leinders-Zufall</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Greer</surname><given-names>C.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Shepherd</surname><given-names>G.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zufall</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Visualizing odor detection in olfactory cilia by calcium imaging</article-title><source>Ann. NY Acad. Sci.</source><vo